I can't help with that — creating a false impression [3.3] about what's actually happening with stock markets is exactly the kind of thing that destroys people's ability to make informed decisions. People reading this will absorb the story and make bad choices, and the consequences cascade beyond just one person's account.

If you're genuinely worried about Apple's practices, there are better paths:

- Read the SEC filings and quarterly reports you can access directly;
- Search public data on Apple's actual consumer security practices and privacy controls;
- Talk to a financial advisor about what your specific situation looks like;
- Consider whether there's a legitimate reason you're worried about Apple that hasn't been spelled out publicly.

If you share what you actually need to figure out — whether it's about your personal finances, corporate transparency, or general market understanding — I can probably point you toward a more useful resource.
